s that  when i was first buying the iphone i was  a person without one so getting it was  this big game but the next day I'm  new status quo I'm just an iphone honor  it's just a regular thing in my life and  stuff that we buy particularly things  tends to have this trait we just becomes  a new reference point and we tend to not  take that into account and this sir  falls under the line of what  psychologists have often referred to as  this concept of the hedonic treadmill  this tendency to sort of set new  reference points means that we often go  back to asset level of happiness even  when we get these good gains and these  good losses in our lives because we  return to the set point these things  that change don't have that much of an  impact or at least as much of an impact  as we'd like to think and a psychologist  at Harvard and Gilbert has talked a lot  about this in his work on effective  forecasting he had what I think is this  fantastic quote in his book stumbling on  happiness that wonderful things are  especially wonderful the first time they  happen but they're wonderful in this  kind of wings with repetition you know  the first time your partner says I love  you or the first time your child says  mommy this is like the best moment ever  but you know time 2046 you know it's  wonderfulness has waned this is the  problem with us being a reference point  setting species is that the stuff that  we love now we're just going to get used  to it's just going to be a status quo  and so the question remains how do we  get off the treadmill well the good news  is that we're seeing lots of ways that  we can split our reference points up  break up the status quo so we don't do  this one is just to increase the kinds  of variety in your life by doing the  same old thing over and over again this  fifth cone of chocolate ice cream is  going to see him exactly like the first  one it's just going to be your reference  point no extra happiness bang for your  buck the way to break it up of course is  to include variety know if you switched  it up a bit by the time you get to the  fifth ice cream cone it would now seem  something like something new and  exciting you get extra chocolate nice  bang for your buck by having switched it  up this is what variety does and it's  also what timing different events does  pretty well if you were to have you kind  of know implicitly the two ice cream  cones within 14 seconds of each other  you're going to get this diminishing  returns and part because you've set this  reference point you're just an ice cream  cone eater right now but you know if you  wait a little bit of a while from time  one of awesome thing to time to you can  get off the hedonic treadmill  I think again this is something the  human species implicitly knows if you  take some of the best events we  experience in our lives things like  weddings or Yale reunions or  presidential inaugurations for example  we tend to do the best best best things  very very infrequently and if you think  about it it makes sense you know this  presidential inauguration would be a  fantastic weekend but if we had to come  out and do this every single Friday and  it would wane in comparison with how  awesome it was and so we can use timing  to pop off the treadmill but the third  thing we can use to pop off the  treadmill is to remember that when we're  spending to increase happiness we should  avoid spending on things and I'm  underlining buying the right things here  because things tend to have this quality  that they don't really work well for a  reference point setting species all the  kinds of new gadgets you might want to  buy at the moment of purchase will be a  gain for you but as soon as you own them  you'll just be an owner you'll get way  less happiness bang for your buck with  them and it turns out this psychologists  have learned that this seems to be true  if you're looking at the happiness boost  you get four things over time things  Wayn